Since mid-July, Ukraine has been targeting warehouses of Russia's largest online retailer Wildberries almost every day. Ukraine says it's disrupting front-line shipments. But experts say it's disrupting Russia's economy.

Early Sunday, a fire broke out in a warehouse belonging to Russia's largest online marketplace , Wildberries,&#160;in the town of Podolsk, about 40 kilometers (25 miles) south of Moscow. The fire was the result of a&#160;large-scale overnight attack by Ukrainian drones , most of which were intercepted by Russian air defenses.

On Friday,&#160; Ukraine struck a different Wildberries warehouse, in Tver, north of Moscow. The day before, it had set fire to another&#160;warehouse in Salavat, further east.

About 20 Wildberries logistics hubs have been under near-daily attack in the past few weeks . At least nine employees have been confirmed dead, and scores injured, while a handful of the storehouses have&#160;halted operations.&#160;

The Moscow-based e-commerce consultancy Data Insight estimates Wildberries has lost as much as 480 billion rubles worth of goods ($5.7 billion/€4.9 billion) and about a third of its warehouse space. Combined damaged to the company and its sellers are estimated as high as 800 billion rubles ($9.5 billion).

While Kyiv argues&#160;the retailer helps supply Russia's military , Moscow maintains it serves only civilian purposes. Either way, the attacks have been placing an increased strain on Russia's economy.&#160;

"I think [Ukraine is]&#160;going after a choke point," Fabrice Pothier, CEO of the geopolitical consultancy firm Rasmussen Global, told DW. "Wildberries is, in a way, a choke point of Russian society and the Russian economy."

Following Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022 and the ensuing sanctions on Russian trade , many online e-commerce platforms such as Amazon withdrew from the region. In response, Russian authorities permitted so-called parallel imports, essentially allowing Russian companies to sidestep sanctions by importing goods without the consent of the original rights holders.&#160;

These companies drastically undercut conventional prices, allowing their sales volume to skyrocket. In the case of Wildberries, for example, revenue increased sevenfold between 2021 and 2025. Its competitor, Ozon, offers similarly staggering numbers, with a reported $49.3 million revenue and 59% growth in 2025.

Today, Wildberries is the largest e-commerce platform in the country, controlling about half of the respective market. Ozon dominates about 30% of the market. Together, they handle goods and services equivalent to about 8.5% of the Russia's gross domestic product (GDP).&#160;

